#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Synthesize an Evolution gate in fermionic space for the latest version of "
"Qiskit Fermions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Synthesize an Evolution gate in fermionic space"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"fermionic-synthesis-explanation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Synthesize an Evolution gate in fermionic space {#synthesize-an-evolution-"
"gate-in-fermionic-space}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  The concepts in this guide are currently available only in the Python API. Equivalent functionality will be made available in the C API in a future release.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"An [`Evolution`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-"
"library-evolution#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution\") gate carries the time "
"evolution $e^{-itH}$ of a whole operator. A fermion-to-fermion synthesis "
"rewrites that gate into a "
"[`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") of smaller fermionic gates. "
"Both sides of the rewrite stay in fermionic space, which is what "
"distinguishes it from the fermion-to-qubit synthesis that follows: the "
"operator’s [`groups`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.groups "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.groups\") (see [Group operator "
"terms: use the operator structure](grouping#grouping-explanation)) survive "
"it, whereas a fermion-to-qubit mapping returns a "
"[`SparseObservable`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.quantum_info.SparseObservable),"
" which has no concept of grouping.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Two knobs select the method. `Evolution.synthesis` sets it on one gate, and "
"the [`F2FSynthesis`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/transpiler-"
"passes-f-2-f-synthesis#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.F2FSynthesis "
"\"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.F2FSynthesis\") transpiler pass sets it "
"across a whole circuit. Both take an "
"[`F2FSynthesisPlugin`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-synthesis-f-2-f-synthesis-"
"plugin#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.F2FSynthesisPlugin "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.F2FSynthesisPlugin\"), the "
"interface any such rewrite implements. The methods this package currently "
"provides for an evolution are product formulas, which is what the rest of "
"this guide uses: "
"[`FermionicLieTrotter`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-synthesis-fermionic-lie-"
"trotter#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicLieTrotter "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicLieTrotter\") for a "
"first-order formula (the default) and "
"[`FermionicSuzukiTrotter`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-synthesis-fermionic-suzuki-"
"trotter#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicSuzukiTrotter "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicSuzukiTrotter\") for a "
"higher even order. Anything else implementing the interface is selected the "
"same way, and the sections on the two knobs apply to it unchanged.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  This fermion-to-fermion step is **optional**. An [`Evolution`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-library-evolution#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution\") gate can go straight to the fermion-to-qubit stage, which maps and synthesizes it whole however many terms it holds. Splitting it first is a choice, made because the resulting gates are individually cheaper or because the split exposes structure that a later stage can use.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The two steps compose, so an evolution can end up approximated at either level, at both, or at neither. A product formula approximates unless its factors commute; a synthesis method need not approximate at all.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Skipping this step does not have to mean losing the grouping entirely. Wrapping the mapper in [`group_wise()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/transpiler-passes-synthesis-group-wise#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.synthesis.group_wise \"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.synthesis.group_wise\") maps the operator one group at a time and sums the results, so the mapped Pauli terms arrive grouped rather than interleaved. A qubit-side product formula synthesizes them in the order it receives, so commuting terms that end up adjacent can be scheduled together.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  On the Hamiltonian below that recovers the whole two-qubit depth benefit of splitting: 18 instead of 30, at an unchanged gate count. The difference is *where* the structure is expressed. Splitting in fermionic space makes each group a separate gate, which every later stage can see and act on; `group_wise` leaves one gate and only reorders the terms inside it, which is enough for scheduling but leaves nothing for a fermionic pass to work with. Use it when the evolution has to stay a single gate, and reach for a fermionic product formula otherwise.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Build a grouped Hamiltonian {#build-a-grouped-hamiltonian}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both product formulas split an operator into factors, and for a grouped "
"operator those factors are its groups. The grouping is therefore what "
"determines both the cost and the accuracy of the result. Take a six-site "
"Fermi-Hubbard chain, grouped the way its terms commute: the hopping terms on"
" even bonds, the hopping terms on odd bonds, and the diagonal interaction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Fifteen terms in three groups. Each hopping group pairs every term with its "
"conjugate partner, which matters: a factor has to be Hermitian for its "
"exponential to be unitary, and that does not follow from the sum being "
"Hermitian. "
"[`groups_are_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-terms-groups-are-"
"hermitian#qiskit_fermions.operators.terms.groups_are_hermitian "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.terms.groups_are_hermitian\") checks that "
"convention rather than leaving it to inspection:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Separating $a^\\dagger_0 a_1$ from its partner $a^\\dagger_1 a_0$ would "
"break it, and leaving the operator ungrouped has the same effect, since both"
" formulas then split it *term by term* and a lone $a^\\dagger_i a_j$ is not "
"Hermitian:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Such a factor is a valid fermionic operator, and this package will build the"
" gate for it, so nothing complains until the mapped operator reaches "
"[`PauliEvolutionGate`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.circuit.library.PauliEvolutionGate),"
" which requires real coefficients and raises `ValueError: Operator contains "
"complex coefficients, which are not supported`. Checking the grouping up "
"front turns that late failure into an answer available before any circuit is"
" built.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Set the method on one gate {#set-the-method-on-one-gate}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Pass a synthesis method to the gate, then decompose it. Each gate it emits "
"comes back as its own [`Evolution`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-evolution#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution"
" \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution\") gate:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"First order emits one factor per group. Order two emits the symmetric "
"palindrome, so the two outer groups appear twice at half the time each, "
"giving five. Four repetitions of that would give twenty, but each repetition"
" ends on the same group the next one begins with. Those neighboring gates "
"evolve under the same operator, so they are fused into one gate with the "
"summed time, which saves one gate per seam. Pass `merge_adjacent=False` to "
"keep the textbook sequence. Every order splits the operator the same way; "
"what changes is the ordering of the factors and their evolution times, not "
"the partition.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  The factors are marked `atomic`, so decomposing again leaves them in place rather than splitting them further. The decomposition has a fixed point, which is what makes it safe to call [`decompose()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.decompose \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.decompose\") repeatedly.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Set the method for a whole pipeline {#set-the-method-for-a-whole-pipeline}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Setting `synthesis` per gate means threading the choice through everything "
"that builds one, including [`UCC`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-ucc#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.UCC "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.UCC\") and "
"[`UCJ`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-library-"
"ucj#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.UCJ "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.UCJ\"), which construct their gates "
"internally. The [`F2FSynthesis`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/transpiler-"
"passes-f-2-f-synthesis#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.F2FSynthesis "
"\"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.F2FSynthesis\") pass applies one method "
"to every [`Evolution`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-evolution#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution"
" \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.Evolution\") in a circuit instead, so the"
" choice is made once for the pipeline. It is configured by gate name, the "
"same way [`F2QSynthesis`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/transpiler-"
"passes-f-2-q-synthesis#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.F2QSynthesis "
"\"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.F2QSynthesis\") is, and a gate that is "
"not named is left as it is:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This is the same count "
"[`decompose()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-"
"fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.decompose "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit.decompose\") gives for that "
"method above. The "
"[`MergeEvolutions`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/transpiler-passes-merge-"
"evolutions#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.MergeEvolutions "
"\"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.MergeEvolutions\") pass applies the same"
" fusion to neighboring gates that did not come from one synthesis, such as "
"the seams between consecutive circuits. The pass carries the formula out "
"itself, so no separate expansion step is needed, and running it twice "
"changes nothing, since the factors it emits are `atomic` and so carry no "
"definition of their own.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<DoqAdmonition type=\"note\" title=\"Note\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Pass `apply=False` to only *select* the method and leave the expansion to "
"something else, such as Qiskit’s "
"[`Decompose`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.transpiler.passes.Decompose)"
" (naming the gate as `Decompose(\"Evolution\")` restricts it to the "
"evolutions, leaving the other fermionic gates to their own synthesis "
"plugins).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  This is useful to choose the method for a whole circuit up front while "
"deferring the expansion to the point where it is wanted, for example to "
"inspect the circuit first:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  Be aware of what an unexpanded gate does, though: it reaches the fermion-"
"to-qubit stage whole, where it is mapped without `synthesis` being "
"consulted. That makes for an easy mistake when comparing methods, because "
"every one of them then produces identical output and the comparison silently"
" measures nothing. On the Hamiltonian above, leaving the order-two gate "
"unexpanded collapses the much larger circuit below to the cost of a single "
"mapped evolution, no matter which method was selected.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "</DoqAdmonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Measure the payoff {#measure-the-payoff}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A higher order costs depth, so the question is not whether it is more "
"accurate but whether it is more accurate *per gate*. Two numbers are needed "
"per method: the error of the state it produces, and the two-qubit cost of "
"the circuit that produced it.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The error is measured with [ffsim](https://qiskit-"
"community.github.io/ffsim/), which simulates a "
"[`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") **directly** (see [How this "
"package relates to ffsim](ffsim#ffsim-relationship-explanation)). No "
"fermion-to-qubit mapping is involved, so the comparison isolates the "
"fermionic product formula from anything the mapping or the qubit-side "
"synthesis might contribute. It also works in the fixed-particle-number "
"sector, which is both smaller than the full $2^N$ space and the space the "
"evolution actually stays in.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The two-qubit cost is left out of that table on purpose. It depends on the "
"qubit-side synthesis Qiskit selects, which improves between releases, so the"
" numbers printed here would be a snapshot rather than a property of the "
"product formula. The figure below plots them instead, and what the "
"comparison rests on is asserted as a relation:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Grouped bars of CX count and depth per method, with order two at four "
"repetitions clearly shorter than order one at "
"ten.](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-fermions/fermionic_synthesis-8.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Read the last two methods together, because they are the comparison that "
"matters. Order two with four repetitions is roughly ten times more accurate "
"than order one with ten, and it gets there with fewer entangling gates "
"rather than more, as the figure shows. Spending a gate budget on a higher "
"order beats spending it on more first-order steps here.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"That result is not universal. Increasing "
"[`reps`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-library-"
"synthesis-fermionic-suzuki-"
"trotter#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicSuzukiTrotter.reps"
" \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicSuzukiTrotter.reps\") "
"and increasing [`order`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-synthesis-fermionic-suzuki-"
"trotter#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicSuzukiTrotter.order"
" \"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicSuzukiTrotter.order\")"
" reduce the error at different rates for the same kind of cost, and which "
"wins depends on the operator and on the time being evolved. The two rows "
"above are how to find out for a given problem.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  The fermionic formula composes with the one chosen for the fermion-to-qubit stage (see [`MapperFnEvolutionSynthesis.product_formula`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/transpiler-passes-synthesis-mapper-fn-evolution-synthesis#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.synthesis.MapperFnEvolutionSynthesis.product_formula \"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.synthesis.MapperFnEvolutionSynthesis.product_formula\")). Where both approximate, the accuracy of the result is governed by the weaker of the two, so raising the order here while the qubit-side formula stays first-order buys little.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Restrict the pass to the gates that benefit {#restrict-the-pass-to-the-"
"gates-that-benefit}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A higher order only helps when the factors do not commute. If they all "
"commute, the first-order formula is already exact and the extra factors are "
"pure overhead. A purely diagonal operator makes that concrete, since number "
"operators commute with each other:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Both circuits are exact, so the second spends 80% more entangling gates for "
"nothing. The diagonal Coulomb operators of a "
"[`UCJ`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/circuit-library-"
"ucj#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.UCJ "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.UCJ\") have this shape, which is why "
"[`F2FSynthesis.filter`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/transpiler-"
"passes-f-2-f-synthesis#qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.F2FSynthesis.filter"
" \"qiskit_fermions.transpiler.passes.F2FSynthesis.filter\") exists: it takes"
" a predicate over the "
"[`DAGOpNode`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.dagcircuit.DAGOpNode)"
" and leaves the rejected gates untouched, so one pass can raise the order on"
" the gates that benefit and leave the rest at first order.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A circuit holding both kinds of evolution shows what that saves. The "
"predicate below accepts a gate only when its operator moves particles "
"between modes, which is exactly when the groups fail to commute and a higher"
" order has something to buy:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The gates the filter saves are exactly the ones order two spent on the "
"diagonal evolution, which the diagonal comparison above prices at eight (18 "
"against 10). They bought nothing there, because that evolution is already "
"exact at first order, so dropping them costs no accuracy.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Note\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Compare the two order-two circuits with each other, not against the unexpanded one. Leaving both gates unexpanded sends each to the fermion-to-qubit stage whole, which is the mistake the `apply=False` note above describes: it can land on the same entangling-gate count as the filtered circuit while being a different circuit at a different depth, so reading the filter’s benefit off that number measures nothing.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Caution\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Neither knob verifies that the factors are Hermitian, and an incorrectly grouped operator can look *better* at an even order than at first order while still being wrong, because the symmetrization partially cancels the error. Group conjugate partners together, as `fermi_hubbard_chain` above does.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Next steps {#next-steps}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Read [Group operator terms: use the operator structure](grouping#grouping-"
"explanation) for how the groups these product formulas split on are "
"assigned.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Read [Transpile fermionic circuits](transpilation#transpilation-explanation)"
" for the stages a "
"[`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") passes through.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"See [Simulate 1D Fermi-Hubbard dynamics with flow sets](1-d-fermi-"
"hubbard#d-fermi-hubbard) for a worked case where the fermionic formula "
"supplies the step count that a custom qubit-side synthesis cannot.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Consult [`synthesis`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-synthesis#module-"
"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis\") for the synthesis methods "
"themselves, and [`FermionicQDrift`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-library-synthesis-fermionic-q-"
"drift#qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicQDrift "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.library.synthesis.FermionicQDrift\") for a "
"randomized alternative.\n"
msgstr ""
